From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Your Name <you@example.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Fix MPU region count and enable check for all S32K1xx derivatives

The system MPU code of the S32K148EVB platform hard-coded properties of
the S32K148 and mixed the read-only configuration field CESR[NRGD] into
the enable check:

- `SMPU_ENTRYS` was a fixed 16, although the number of region
  descriptors belongs to the derivative (S32K142/144/146: 8,
  S32K148: 16). It now follows `MPU_RGD_COUNT` of the device header,
  which also declares `IP_MPU->RGD[]`.
- `isGlobalEnabled()` compared CESR against 0x201, that is VLD = 1 and
  NRGD = 2. NRGD reports how many region descriptors the silicon
  implements (Reference Manual Rev. 14, 15.4.2: 0000b = 8, 0001b = 12,
  0010b = 16) and is read-only, so on a derivative with 8 descriptors
  the enabled MPU was reported as disabled and the safe supervisor
  raised MPU_NOT_ENABLED in every cycle. The check now looks at the
  global enable bit CESR[VLD] only.
- `areRegionsConfiguredCorrectly()` had its own constant of 16 regions
  next to `SMPU_ENTRYS`. On a derivative with 8 descriptors the loop
  read RGD[8].WORD3 (0x4000D48C), which is not implemented and answers
  with a bus fault. It now uses `mpu::SMPU_ENTRYS`.
- `setWordInDescriptor()` asserted `slot <= SMPU_ENTRYS`, so
  `slot == SMPU_ENTRYS` wrote past the last descriptor. The bound is
  now `slot < SMPU_ENTRYS`, matching the reject condition of
  `getWordInDescriptor()`.

On the S32K148 nothing changes: MPU_RGD_COUNT is 16 and CESR[VLD] is
part of the old comparison.

The new host test `safeMemoryMpuTest` redirects IP_MPU to a fake
register block and checks the enable bit for the reset values of
MPU_CESR from Table 15-4 as well as the descriptor bounds.

Signed-off-by: Your Name <you@example.com>
